  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1232 AM MST Thu Nov 12 2026\n\nLike a broken record, mild and dry conditions persist across \nthe Cowboy State today. Temperatures remain 10 to 15 degrees \nabove normal in large part due to high pressure that is \ncentered over the Four Corners region. Highs are forecast to \nreach the low to mid 60s east of the Divide and mid to upper 50s\nin the west. After multiple days of similar weather conditions,\nsome change is around the corner for Friday into the weekend.\n\nHigh pressure looks to be gradually ushered east starting \nFriday as a trough begins to near the West Coast. This will be \nthe next weather maker for the upcoming weekend. Unfortunately, \nthis system is already beginning to fall apart and weaken, \nleading to an unorganized nature and lesser impacts. Friday \nwill see winds increase mainly across the Wind Corridor from \nRock Springs to Casper and up into Johnson County. This is the \nresult of southwesterly flow developing ahead of the trough. \nWinds across the previously mentioned area will range from 15 to\n25 mph with gusts of 30 to 40 mph at times. Elevated fire \nweather conditions may be possible during the afternoon on \nFriday but non-critical fuels and RH above 20 percent should \nhinder fire concerns. Temperatures on Friday will still be mild \nacross all of the state with somewhat cooler values for \nSaturday. Precipitation chances begin to increase Saturday \nmainly across western WY as the system begins to move into the \nGreat Basin region. Model guidance is starting to hint at a \nrather lack luster event with only sparse chances for showers \nduring the day Saturday into Sunday morning. The best chances \nwill remain across the higher elevations of western WY with a \nscattered shower or two possible for the lower elevation \nvalleys. Probabilistic guidance currently only shows a 40-70% \nchance of 0.05 of liquid for the higher elevations and a 20-40% \nfor lower elevations through the weekend. So its currently \nlooking like this systems biggest impacts will be breezy winds \nand cooler temperatures with precipitation chances continuing to\ndecrease. The system moves out of the region Sunday with dry \nand mild conditions returning for the start of next week.\n\nLocations east of the Divide will remain mostly if not entirely\ndry, as southwesterly flow will be rather unfavorable. Mild \ntemperatures persist through the weekend with breezy winds \nlingering across the Wind Corridor.\n\nRidging builds back in for the start of next week with mild and\ndry conditions persisting through the first half. Another \ndisturbance looks to possibly move into the region by the middle\nof the week. However with every passing day it continues to \nlook more disorganized and currently looks to be an open wave by\nthe time it moves through. That being said, there could be some\nprecipitation associated with it but overall it will be light \nin nature. Favored locations would be the higher elevations of \nwestern and central WY. Some precipitation cannot be ruled out \nfor the lower elevations but warm temperatures should keep it in\nthe form of rain. \n\nLooking farther ahead into the end of next week and into the \nweekend, there may be another chance for a disturbance. \nConfidence is low this far out with way too much uncertainty to\ngo into further detail but at this time it may be the best \nchance for any notable precipitation if things can come together\nproperly.",
